{
  "extends": "../../packages/config/tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    /*
     * THE PERMISSIVE HALF. This config drives the editor and ts-jest, and the
     * SUITE genuinely runs in Node — the fences read source with `node:fs`, and
     * the manifest test spawns the real build script.
     *
     * It is deliberately NOT the fence. `tsconfig.build.json`, which emits the
     * artifact that actually ships, sets `types: []` so a `src` module reaching
     * for `process` or `Buffer` fails the build. Same split as `vault-web` and
     * `@estate/vault-crypto`, for the same reason: a permissive config for
     * tests, a strict one for the thing that runs in a browser.
     */
    "lib": ["ES2023", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "types": ["node", "jest"],
    "module": "es2022",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    /*
     * The offscreen worker loads vault-crypto by an ABSOLUTE path that resolves
     * against the EXTENSION's own root (`chrome-extension://<id>/lib/…`), so no
     * bare specifier ever reaches the browser and there is no import map for a
     * CSP to admit. Types come from the built declarations — the repo's
     * standing convention for internal packages; the RUNTIME modules are put
     * there by `scripts/copy-vault-crypto.mjs`, from the browser build.
     */
    "paths": {
      "/lib/vault-crypto/index.js": ["../../packages/vault-crypto/dist/index.d.ts"]
    }
  },
  "include": ["src", "test", "jest.config.js"]
}
